{{Card Infobox|Cost=5|Rarity=Legendary|Type=Troop|Arena=Miner's Mine|ReleaseDate=17 June 2024}}{{SubpageNavBox|MergeTactics=yes}}{{Quote|Goblin Machine has two arms for melee and one big rocket for faraway targets. Who let a baby drive this thing?}}

The Goblin Machine is a {{Rarity|Legendary}} [[Cards|card]] that is unlocked from the [[Arenas|Miner's Mine (Arena 15)]]. It spawns a single-target, ground-targeting, melee, ground troop with high hitpoints and moderate damage. It also carries a rocket launcher on its back that can attack independently from the Goblin Machine. The rocket launcher is an area-damage, air-targeting, medium-ranged troop with very high damage. It has a blind spot, preventing it from attacking enemies inside it. It is a wood and metal mech with team-colored zig zag lines, spikes on the edges, a [[Goblins|Goblin]] emblem in the center, and a team-colored rocket launcher on its back that has a striped team-colored rocket, all controlled by a [[Baby Goblins|Baby Goblin]] who has a magenta mohawk, carries a small hammer on his back, and wears light brown gloves. A Goblin Machine card costs 5 [[Elixir]] to deploy.

==Strategy==
*The Goblin Machine is a valuable tank due to his high damage rocket, making him great on defense against smaller pushes and supporting larger pushes on offense.
*The Goblin Machine should almost never be ignored, as doing so will easily destroy over half of the [[:Category:Crown Towers|Crown Tower]]. This goes for all Tower Troops, but especially the [[Royal Chef]] and [[Dagger Duchess]].
*High damage, high health, single-targeting melee troops, such as [[P.E.K.K.A.|P.E.K.K.A]], [[Mighty Miner]], [[Elite Barbarians]], [[Prince]], [[Monk]], [[Lumberjack]], [[Giant Skeleton]], and [[Mini P.E.K.K.A.|Mini P.E.K.K.A]], are the most effective counters to the Goblin Machine as they’ll do enough DPS to defeat the Goblin Machine with the help of a Crown Tower, stay out of the Rocket’s range, and will counterattack with their remaining hitpoints.
**When using melee troops to defend against a Goblin Machine, it’s optimal to deploy them at least 5 tiles away from the Crown Tower to prevent the Rocket from getting free chip damage onto the Crown Tower.
**Conversely, most ranged cards with a range less than 6 tiles, such as the [[Hunter]], [[Executioner]], [[Bowler]], [[Baby Dragon]], [[Goblin Demolisher]], [[Witch]], [[Archer Queen]], [[Electro Dragon]], [[Cannon Cart]], [[Skeleton Dragons]], and [[Wizard]], are very weak to the Goblin Machine as his Rocket will likely lock onto these cards and quickly destroy them, providing little to no defensive value to the user.
*With the assistance of the Dagger Duchess and an [[Ice Spirit]] or [[Electro Spirit]], a [[Void]] can fully counter the Goblin Machine for a +1 positive Elixir trade. The Void must be played 1 tile in front of the Goblin Machine to achieve all 3 hits while the Ice Sprit should ideally be deployed 4 tiles from the bridge horizontally to buy time for the Dagger Duchess. Failing to do so will allow one Rocket to connect to the Crown Tower, dealing chip damage.
*The Goblin Machine has a notable synergy with the [[Goblin Drill]]. The Rockets coming from the Goblin Machine will likely lock onto anything used by the opponent to defend the Goblin Drill, instantly destroying most swarm troops, like [[Goblin Gang]] and [[Skeleton Army]], and potentially hitting the Crown Tower thanks to its area damage. Also, the Goblin Machine’s high hitpoints can tank the Crown Tower’s attacks for the Goblin Drill’s duration, leaving the [[Goblins]] unscathed to deal significant damage.
**A similar and more deadly strategy works with the [[Goblin Drill/Evolution|Evolved Goblin Drill]] thanks to the Evolved Goblin Drill ability to reappear in a different spot than before; this will likely disorient the opponent from defending properly more so than the regular Goblin Drill.
*Surprisingly, [[Fisherman]] can activate the [[King's Tower|King Tower]] against the Goblin Machine. Wait for the Goblin Machine to come close to the Crown Towers, then place him 2 tiles in front of the King Tower, where he will fire a Rocket at the King Tower, activating it.
*Using air swarms, such as [[Minion Horde]] and [[Bats/Evolution|Evolved Bats]], on offense against a defending Goblin Machine is generally unfavorable as not placing them directly on top of the Goblin Machine will cause the Rocket to target them, wiping out a large amount of troops in 1 hit and essentially wasting the user’s Elixir.
*Due to the Goblin Machine’s Rocket, the [[Goblin Curse]] can be an important synergy as the damage amplification allows the Rocket to take out [[Magic Archer]], [[Mother Witch]], [[Zappies]], [[Flying Machine]], and [[Skeleton Dragons]] all in 1 hit instead of 2. Also, the Goblin Machine will tank for the spawned Goblins, forcing an immediate response from the opponent.
*Be weary when trying to kite the Goblin Machine into the other lane with a building-targeting troop like [[Ice Golem]] or [[Goblin Giant]]. Dropping the troop too far away from the bridge can result in a Rocket locking onto the Crown Tower and dealing chip damage.
*Due to the rocket’s slow projectile speed, troops with a Very Fast movement speed can actually out run the rocket shot by the Goblin Machine under most circumstances. This is important in situations where the Goblin Machine is distracted by a slow card while a faster card is targeted by the rocket, such as an Ice Golem + [[Hog Rider]] push.
*In [[2v2]] [[Basics of Battle|battles]], if the Goblin Machine locks onto a Crown Tower, the rocket will target the King's Tower. If it is crossing the bridge alone and headed for the healthier Crown Tower, it may be valuable to let it connect to the Crown Tower before spending Elixir to take it down. This also means that it may not be worth it to use it in 2v2 Battle decks, as a King activation can severely cripple future pushes.

